decisions:
  - id: D-agent-taxonomy
    decision: '7-tier agent taxonomy: Builders, Reviewers, Strategists, Intelligence, Infrastructure, Meta, Human Ops'
    rationale: 'Natural clustering by function, not domain. An agent''s tier determines how the orchestrator uses it: builders produce, reviewers validate, strategists decide, intelligence gathers, infrastructure enables, meta manages agents, human ops serves the person.'
  - id: D-notebook-bootstrap
    decision: Bootstrap agent notebooks from canonical sources rather than waiting for session-based learning
    rationale: Agents with empty notebooks are generalists pretending to be specialists. Bootstrapping from Material Design 3, OWASP, Playwright docs etc gives them real knowledge from day one. The learning loop then evolves and adds project-specific patterns on top.
  - id: D-pods-are-platform
    decision: Agent pods (team recommendations) are a nevr.land registry feature, not agent behavior
    rationale: Pods emerge from collaboration graphs in agent profiles. The marketplace surfaces them as install recommendations. This keeps agent profiles clean (they know who they pair with) while the platform handles team composition.
  - id: D-54-not-20
    decision: Grew from 20 to 54 agents by following genuine capability gaps rather than arbitrary limits
    rationale: Started with core engineering (8), added business/design (12), then meta/generalists (Jinx, Sunday, Loid), then deep specialists (WebRTC, AI/ML, mobile, a11y), then business figures (Hormozi, Leila). Each addition filled a real gap no existing agent covered.
  - id: D-hormozi-agents
    decision: Sales agent (Mozi) modeled on Alex Hormozi methodology, operations agent (Leila) modeled on Leila Hormozi
    rationale: These aren't generic business agents. They encode specific frameworks ($100M Offers value equation, CLOSER sales framework, Core Four lead gen, EOS operating system) that the user actively uses in their businesses.
learnings:
  - Agent notebooks need bootstrapping from canonical sources — the learning loop alone takes too many sessions to reach useful knowledge density
  - Mika (designer) at 59 entries is the proof that deep bootstrapping works — color theory, typography, iconography, M3, HIG, WCAG, Radix, Tailwind all distilled into actionable patterns
  - The collaboration graph (pairs_well_with) is the most important field in an agent profile — it determines how the orchestrator composes teams
  - Agent personality (style, risk, verbosity) shapes communication more than expertise — Jinx being confrontational and Sunday being warm are as important as their knowledge
  - Named agents (nicknames) create identity and make the team feel real — 'Ask Mika' is more natural than 'run the designer agent'
  - 54 agents is not too many — the orchestrator only activates the subset needed per task. Having specialists available is better than generalists trying to cover gaps
  - Pod recommendations should emerge from existing collaboration data, not be manually curated — the pairs_well_with graph already encodes which agents work together
